-- chem/transport.hypercubical — the magnetic loops conserve each vertex's net charge, and transport along the
-- graph moves flux between a vertex's two link-edges without changing its charge or either link's flux.
--
-- A vertex's net charge is the sum of its innermost labels, net es = Σ_e (fst (end e)). A single loop α_{jk}^{±s}
-- pushes +s onto end j and −s onto end k (chem/magloop.hypercubical, loop-act), so it adds s and −s to the same
-- sum: net is loop-invariant. The magnetic operator is a sum of such loops at one vertex, so it never changes that
-- vertex's charge — the repo's magnetic term moves no charge between vertices (chem/magnetic.hypercubical). What a
-- loop does move is the innermost flux between two of the vertex's own edges. A transport loop at a vertex v on the
-- path, run between v's edge toward the previous vertex and its edge toward the next, carries one unit of that flux
-- across v: v's charge stays, the two link-edges' innermost labels change by ∓1, and the flux each link carries to
-- its neighbour (beyond) is untouched (loop-keeps-beyond). This is the step that an inter-vertex interaction is
-- built from: charge conserved everywhere, flux handed along the links.
import "magloop.hypercubical"

